; bfsodium TUPLEHASH256 : NIST SP 800_185 section 5's TupleHash
;
; NOTE square brackets are brainfuck loops  so comments use braces for counts;
;
; HAND WRITTEN skeleton; keccak/bytepad136 is PASTED once  keccak/leftenc
; 5 times  chacha20/add32 5 times  keccak/rightenc once  and
; keccak/sponge136 once; This file adds no cryptography of its own;
;
; IO  in:  xof{1}  u{4}  olen{2} LE  slen{2} LE  mlen{2} LE
;          elen{4}  sbuf{128}  ebuf{256}    (399 bytes)
;          then mlen bytes from the wire
;     out: olen bytes
;
;   TupleHash256(X  L  S) = cSHAKE256( encode_string of every element of
;                          X in turn  then right_encode(L)
;                          L  "TupleHash"  S )
;
; THE TUPLE IS THE HARD PART AND PREPENDING IS THE ANSWER; the encoding puts
; every element behind a length that the elements BEFORE it decide  which is
; an offset  which is an index  and this library has none; keccak/bytepad met
; the same wall and went through it the same way: BUILD THE ANSWER RIGHT TO
; LEFT; each piece is put in by sliding the whole encoding right by its own
; length and ADDING it at the encoding's first cell  and CELL NOUGHT IS ALWAYS
; CELL NOUGHT;
;
; WHICH IS WHY THE ELEMENTS ARE VISITED BACKWARDS  and why there are exactly
; 4 of them: a backwards walk over a list needs an index to find its end  so
; the 4 tape slots are UNROLLED  each behind a flag of its own; u{4} says
; which slots carry an element  and a slot's length may be nought because an
; EMPTY ELEMENT IS A REAL ELEMENT in this standard and encodes to two bytes;
;
; THE LAST ELEMENT COMES FROM THE WIRE AND HAS NO LIMIT; only its LENGTH is on
; the tape  encoded and prepended before any slot  because it is the last
; thing in the answer and so the first thing prepended; So the tuple is the 4
; slots and then the wire  which is up to 5 elements  and only the first 4
; of them are bounded; A caller with one long element puts it on the wire;
;
; EACH SLOT IS AT MOST 64 BYTES and SLEN AT MOST 119  and the routine DOES
; NOT CHECK EITHER; the encoding buffer is 272 cells  which is exactly the
; worst the 4 slots and the wire length can come to  so a slot over its
; limit pushes bytes off the end rather than growing it;
;
; XOF IS ONE CELL AND NOT A SECOND FILE  exactly as in keccak/kmac256:
; TupleHashXOF is TupleHash with right_encode(0) behind the message  and
; right_encode of nought is what the encoder produces when handed nought  so
; the flag CLEARS THE ENCODER'S INPUT rather than branching around it;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000        pad     the padding byte the sponge is handed  which is 4
;   @0x001:0x002  mlen{2} the message length  little endian
;   @0x003:0x004  olen{2} the output length
;   @0x005:0x006  plen{2} one rate for cSHAKE's block plus the encoding
;   @0x007:0x226  the sponge's own prefix staging
;   @0x227:0x228  the sponge's suffix length  and
;   @0x229:0x230  its suffix buffer
;   @0x000:0x637  keccak/sponge136 pasted at this file's own zero
;   @0x640:0x803  keccak/bytepad136's frame  pasted at @0x640
;   @0x808:0x996  everything read  parked above both frames
;   @0x99f:0xaae  the tuple encoding  built from its right hand end
;   @0xaaf        k       how far the next prepend slides
;   @0xab0:0xab1  the same count  kept to slide by and to total
;   @0xab2:0xab3  zlen{2} how long the encoding has become
;   @0xab4:0xab6  the cells the tests are made through
;   @0xab7        flag    the slot's own flag  as it is tested
;   @0xabb:0xacc  keccak/leftenc's frame; footprint 0:17
;   @0xacf:0xae2  chacha20/add32's frame; footprint 0:19
;   @0xae3:0xae6  tmp{4}  hands the accumulator back to itself
;   @0xae7        dbl     the three doublings still to do
;   @0xae8        xofc    the flag  as the clear reads it
;   @0xaeb:0xafc  keccak/rightenc's frame; footprint 0:17

; read the flags  the lengths  the customization string and the slots
,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
